    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i checked the logs but it's hard to tell if it's a race condition or if the old process is just hanging around. the port error happens almost immediately after the pod tries to start up. \n\nif i want to rule out resource constraints like OOMKilled or CPU throttling, what specific metrics in stackdriver (cloud monitoring) should i be looking at to see if the pod is hitting its limits right before it crashes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To check for resource constraints, focus on two key metrics in Cloud Monitoring for your node and the pod. First, monitor `container_cpu_usage_time` and `container_memory_usage_bytes` to see if the pod usage is consistently hitting or exceeding the `limits` you defined in the deployment. Second, check `kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total` to confirm the restarts are due to the port error, not resource pressure.",
      "source": "target",
